<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A worked example: the default refinance</h2>
    <p>The calculator starts with $285,000 left on a 6.75% loan with 27 years to go, a payment of $1,914.06. Refinancing to 5.75% with $6,500 in closing costs gives very different results depending on the new term:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th class="num">Keep current loan</th><th class="num">New 30-year</th><th class="num">New 27-year</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Monthly P&amp;I</td><td class="num">$1,914.06</td><td class="num">$1,663.18</td><td class="num">$1,734.14</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Monthly savings</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">$250.88</td><td class="num">$179.92</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Break-even on $6,500</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">25.9 months</td><td class="num">36.1 months</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Interest still to pay</td><td class="num">$335,156</td><td class="num">$313,746</td><td class="num">$276,860</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Net savings after costs</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">$14,910</td><td class="num">$51,796</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The 30-year option looks best on a monthly basis, but it adds 3 years of payments. Keeping the payoff date at 27 years (enter 27 as the new term) saves $36,886 more over the life of the loan. The monthly payment is the number lenders lead with; total interest is the one that tells you whether the refinance actually saves money.</p>

    <h2>The best of both: refinance, then keep paying the old amount</h2>
    <p>Take the 30-year loan for its lower required payment, but keep paying the old $1,914.06. The extra $250.88 goes to principal each month, and the loan is paid off in about 21 years and 10 months with roughly $215,500 in total interest. That beats both options in the table, while the lower required payment stays available as a safety net. Use the loan calculator's "extra principal" field to test your own numbers.</p>

    <h2>Is the break-even point realistic for you?</h2>
    <p>Break-even is closing costs divided by monthly savings: $6,500 ÷ $250.88 = 25.9 months. If you might sell or refinance again before then, the new loan loses money. Things that move the break-even:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rolled-in costs.</strong> Adding closing costs to the loan avoids paying cash but means paying interest on them for decades.</li>
      <li><strong>Discount points.</strong> Each point costs 1% of the loan to buy a lower rate. Points make sense only if you keep the loan well past their own break-even.</li>
      <li><strong>Escrow.</strong> A refinance often requires funding a new escrow account; your old servicer refunds the old balance a few weeks later. This is timing, not cost, but it affects the cash you need at closing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Cash-out refinances</h2>
    <p>Cash-out adds to the new balance, so the payment may not fall at all. It can make sense to replace much higher-rate debt, but it turns short-term borrowing into a 30-year obligation secured by your home. Compare the total interest on the cash portion with the alternatives, such as a home equity line or paying the debt down directly.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Refinance questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How do I calculate the break-even point on a refinance?</summary><p>Divide the total upfront cost (closing costs plus any points) by the monthly payment savings. $6,500 in costs and $250.88 in monthly savings break even in about 26 months.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does refinancing to a new 30-year loan cost more?</summary><p>It can. Restarting the clock adds years of payments. In the example, a new 30-year loan saves $14,910 overall, while matching the 27 years left on the old loan saves $51,796.</p></details>
    <details><summary>When is refinancing not worth it?</summary><p>When you expect to sell or refinance again before the break-even point, when the new term adds enough years to erase the interest savings, or when closing costs are high relative to the payment reduction.</p></details>
  </section>
